    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'Which advanced, FDA-approved neurostimulation therapy has revolutionized the treatment of moderate-to-severe OSA by actively stenting open the retrolingual airway during inspiration?', '["Phrenic nerve stimulation", "Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation (Inspire therapy)", "Vagus nerve stimulation", "Deep brain stimulation", "Glossopharyngeal nerve ablation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Hypoglossal nerve stimulation uses an implantable system that senses the patient''s inspiratory effort and delivers a mild electrical pulse to the hypoglossal nerve, causing the tongue to protrude and open the hypopharyngeal airway.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the FDA-approved AHI criteria range for a patient to qualify for Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation?', '["AHI 5 to 15", "AHI 15 to 65", "AHI greater than 100", "Only for an AHI of zero", "AHI 30 to 45 only"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Inspire is indicated for adult patients (age ≥ 18) with moderate-to-severe OSA (AHI 15 to 65) who have failed or cannot tolerate CPAP.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Aside from AHI, what is the standard Body Mass Index (BMI) threshold recommended to ensure high success rates for Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation?', '["BMI less than 32 to 35 kg/m2", "BMI greater than 45 kg/m2", "There is no BMI limit", "BMI less than 18 kg/m2", "Morbid obesity is required"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In highly obese patients (BMI > 32-35), the airway is crowded by global adipose tissue. The mechanical protrusion of the tongue is often insufficient to overcome this circumferential bulk, leading to poor surgical outcomes.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During the pre-operative DISE required for Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation, what specific airway collapse pattern is considered an ABSOLUTE contraindication for the device?', '["Anteroposterior (AP) tongue base collapse", "Complete concentric collapse (CCC) of the velum (soft palate)", "Lateral epiglottic collapse", "AP epiglottic collapse", "No collapse at all"]'::jsonb, 1, 'DISE is mandatory before Inspire implantation. If the soft palate collapses in a sphincter-like, concentric pattern (CCC), the device will not work. Inspire requires an AP collapse pattern to successfully open the retropalatal space via the palatoglossus/genioglossus connection.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which specific anatomical branch of the hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) must be carefully isolated and stimulated, while excluding others, during Inspire surgery?', '["The lateral branch (retractor fibers)", "The medial branch (protrusor fibers supplying the genioglossus and geniohyoid muscles)", "The descending branch", "The main trunk before branching", "The branches to the hyoglossus and styloglossus muscles"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The hypoglossal nerve splits into a medial branch (protrusion/tongue opening) and a lateral branch (retraction/tongue closing). The surgeon must place the cuff electrode ONLY on the medial protrusion fibers. Accidental stimulation of the lateral branch will pull the tongue backward, worsening the sleep apnea.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which muscles are innervated by the lateral branch of the hypoglossal nerve and must be EXCLUDED from stimulation during Inspire surgery?', '["Genioglossus and geniohyoid", "Hyoglossus and styloglossus", "Cricothyroid and vocalis", "Palatopharyngeus and palatoglossus", "Mylohyoid and digastric"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The hyoglossus and styloglossus muscles pull the tongue back and upward (retraction). Excluding these fibers ensures that the electrical pulse selectively pushes the tongue forward.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What are the three main components surgically implanted during a Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation (Inspire) procedure?', '["An oxygen tank, a mask, and a hose", "An Implantable Pulse Generator (IPG) in the chest pocket, a stimulation lead (cuff) on the hypoglossal nerve, and a respiration sensing lead in the intercostal space", "A brain electrode, a neck cuff, and a battery", "A tracheostomy tube, a speaker, and a remote", "A jaw plate, a tongue suture, and a battery"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The sensing lead (placed between the internal and external intercostal muscles) detects when the patient inhales. It signals the IPG (similar to a pacemaker) in the right chest pocket, which instantly fires the stimulation lead cuff on the neck nerve to protrude the tongue during inhalation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which surgical procedure is considered the single most effective non-tracheostomy surgery for severe Obstructive Sleep Apnea, boasting success rates over 80-90%?', '["Standard UPPP", "Maxillomandibular Advancement (MMA)", "Genioglossus advancement", "Midline glossectomy", "Nasal septoplasty"]'::jsonb, 1, 'MMA is a massive skeletal procedure that expands the entire bony framework of the upper airway. By pulling the skeletal attachments of the palate and tongue forward, it enlarges both the retropalatal and retrolingual airways simultaneously.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What are the two standard skeletal osteotomies (bony cuts) performed during a Maxillomandibular Advancement (MMA) surgery?', '["A Le Fort I osteotomy of the maxilla and bilateral sagittal split osteotomies (BSSO) of the mandible", "A Le Fort III osteotomy and a mandibular symphyseal split", "A condylar resection and a palatal split", "A hyoid suspension and a genioglossus split", "A craniotomy and a jaw resection"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The maxilla is mobilized via a Le Fort I cut, and the mandible is mobilized via bilateral sagittal split osteotomies (BSSO). Both jaws are then advanced forward, typically by 10-12 mm, and secured with titanium plates.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How many millimeters of skeletal advancement is generally targeted during a Maxillomandibular Advancement (MMA) to ensure maximum airway expansion?', '["1 to 2 mm", "5 mm", "10 to 12 mm", "At least 30 mm", "Jaw advancement is not measured in millimeters"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Research shows that advancements less than 10 mm have significantly lower success rates. Advancing the skeleton by 10-12 mm physically pulls the tongue, palate, and lateral walls forward, creating a spacious pharyngeal airway.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient undergoing MMA is highly concerned about how their face will look after the surgery. What is the classic aesthetic outcome of this procedure?', '["They will look disfigured", "A subtle, often highly favorable aesthetic improvement, as advancing the jaws corrects retrognathia and tightens loose submental skin (the \"double chin\"), though a minor over-projection of the lower face can occur", "Their face will become completely round", "Their nose will shrink", "Their eyes will move closer together"]'::jsonb, 1, 'MMA is essentially a double orthognathic advancement. In retrognathic patients, it restores normal jaw harmony and dramatically tightens floppy neck tissue, improving their profile while curing their OSA.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which cranial nerve is at the highest risk of temporary or permanent sensory injury during the bilateral sagittal split osteotomy (BSSO) of the mandible in an MMA?', '["The Facial nerve (CN VII)", "The Inferior Alveolar Nerve (a branch of CN V3)", "The Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)", "The Vagus nerve (CN X)", "The Lingual nerve"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The inferior alveolar nerve runs directly through the mandibular canal, exactly where the BSSO bone cuts are made. Transient numbness of the lower lip and chin (paresthesia) is extremely common post-operatively, resolving over 3-6 months. Permanent numbness occurs in a small percentage of cases.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the "Genioglossus Advancement" (GA) skeletal procedure?', '["The complete removal of the tongue", "A procedure where a rectangular window of bone containing the genial tubercle (where the genioglossus muscle attaches) is cut from the front of the chin, pulled forward, and rotated to lock it in place, tensing the tongue base", "The surgical stretching of the tongue", "A type of dental implant", "The injection of calcium into the tongue"]'::jsonb, 1, 'GA tenses the base of the tongue without changing the dental bite. By pulling the bony attachment of the genioglossus forward by about 1 cm, it prevents the tongue from falling backward during sleep. It is often combined with UPPP.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is "Hyoid Suspension" (hyoidopectomy) in sleep surgery?', '["The surgical removal of the hyoid bone", "A procedure where the hyoid bone is surgically mobilized and suspended forward and upward to the thyroid cartilage or the mandible, stabilizing the hypopharyngeal airway", "A type of neck dissection", "A surgery to lower the voice", "A treatment for neck arthritis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Since the hyoid bone anchors the tongue base and epiglottis, suspending the hyoid bone anteriorly and superiorly prevents retrolingual collapse. It is a highly effective "middle-level" airway stabilizer.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'In patients with severe retrolingual obstruction due to a massive tongue base, what advanced surgical technique uses a robot to perform a precise, volumetric reduction of the tongue muscle?', '["Transoral Laser Microsurgery (TOLM)", "Transoral Robotic Surgery (TORS) Midline Glossectomy", "Robotic neck dissection", "Robotic UPPP", "Robotic jaw advancement"]'::jsonb, 1, 'TORS midline glossectomy allows the surgeon to use the DaVinci robot to safely and precisely excise a wedge of muscle and lingual tonsil tissue from the deep midline tongue base, widening the hypopharyngeal airway. It is highly effective but carries a recovery marked by significant pain and temporary swallowing difficulty.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the most common, feared acute complication following a TORS midline glossectomy or any major tongue base reduction surgery?', '["Loss of teeth", "Delayed severe hemorrhage from the lingual artery branches, which can cause rapid airway obstruction", "Vocal cord paralysis", "Taste loss that is always permanent", "Tracheoesophageal fistula"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The tongue base is highly vascular, supplied by the large lingual arteries. A delayed bleed deep in the throat can quickly form a massive hematoma or drown the patient in blood, representing a catastrophic airway emergency.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the "surgical cure rate" of a tracheostomy in patients with severe, life-threatening, refractory Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["Approximately 50 percent", "Nearly 100 percent, representing the ultimate, definitive cure because it completely bypasses the entire upper airway obstruction", "Less than 10 percent", "0 percent; it does not work", "Tracheostomy is strictly contraindicated in OSA"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A tracheostomy is 100% curative because the breathing hole is placed below the site of collapse (the throat). However, due to its massive impact on quality of life, hygiene, and social stigma, it is reserved strictly as a last resort for life-threatening cases that have failed every other option.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old morbidly obese patient (BMI = 52) with an AHI of 110 and severe oxygen nadir of 50 percent has failed CPAP, BiPAP, ASV, and oral appliances. They are in chronic right-sided heart failure. What is the most appropriate next step to secure their airway and save their life?', '["A standard UPPP", "Surgical Tracheostomy (with a customized long or adjustable tube to accommodate their thick neck)", "Hypoglossal nerve stimulation", "Nasal septoplasty", "Diet pills only"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is a classic "life-saving" indication for tracheostomy. Morbid obesity makes other surgeries highly dangerous or ineffective, and their severe hypoxia and heart failure require immediate, guaranteed airway patency.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the "multilevel surgery" philosophy in modern sleep surgery?', '["Performing the same UPPP surgery multiple times", "Identifying the specific sites of collapse (palatal, lateral wall, tongue base, epiglottis) using DISE or exam, and combining targeted procedures (e.g., ESP + GA + Hyoid suspension) in a single or staged setting to address all sites", "Using multiple different brands of CPAP", "Using medicine and CPAP together", "Performing surgery on the nose, throat, and lungs"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Because most adult OSA patients have multi-level collapse, doing a single-site surgery (like UPPP) often fails. Modern sleep surgeons custom-tailor a package of surgeries to address the exact levels of obstruction identified.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During an MMA, why is "Rigid Internal Fixation" (using titanium plates and screws) preferred over wiring the jaw shut (maxillomandibular fixation) post-operatively?', '["Wiring the jaw shut is too expensive", "Rigid fixation holds the bone cuts securely, allowing the patient to open their mouth, speak, and eat a soft diet immediately post-op, which drastically improves nutrition, breathing, and comfort", "Rigid fixation is temporary", "Wiring the jaw shut causes vocal cord paralysis", "Rigid fixation allows the bones to move"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Modern OMFS uses solid titanium plates. In the past, patients had their jaws wired shut for 6 weeks, which was miserable, restricted breathing in OSA patients, and carried a risk of aspiration. Rigid fixation has made MMA much safer and more tolerable.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient undergoing MMA is found to have a severe class II malocclusion (overbite). How does the surgeon manage their dental alignment during the skeletal advancement?', '["The teeth are completely removed", "The surgeon works with an orthodontist who places braces before surgery to align the teeth, ensuring that when the jaws are advanced, the patient''s bite locks into a perfect, functional alignment", "The jaw is advanced without regard for the teeth", "The teeth are filed down", "A bridge is placed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'MMA is a highly coordinated orthodontic and surgical effort. Pre-operative braces align the arches so that the surgical jaw jump results in a stable, normal bite (occlusion).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the primary sensory innervation of the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, which runs near the mandibular BSSO cuts during MMA?', '["The Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)", "The Lingual Nerve (a branch of CN V3)", "The Facial nerve (CN VII)", "The Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)", "The Vagus nerve"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The lingual nerve provides general sensation to the front of the tongue. It runs close to the medial aspect of the mandible and can be stretched or injured during BSSO, causing temporary or permanent tongue numbness or taste changes (via chorda tympani).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which surgical procedure is a skeletal alternative to MMA for pediatric patients with severe retrognathia, involving placing adjustable devices on the jaw bones and slowly expanding them by 1 mm a day to grow the bone?', '["Laryngotracheal reconstruction", "Distraction Osteogenesis (Mandibular distraction)", "Cricotracheal resection", "Rapid maxillary expansion", "Tracheostomy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Mandibular distraction osteogenesis (MDO) is highly effective in infants with Pierre Robin sequence or craniofacial syndromes. It avoids a tracheostomy by slowly stretching the jaw forward, growing new bone and pulling the tongue base out of the throat.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the primary post-operative diet restriction for a patient who has just undergone Maxillomandibular Advancement?', '["NPO (nothing by mouth) for 1 month", "A strict liquid or pureed (no-chew) diet for 4 to 6 weeks to allow the bony osteotomies to fully heal and fuse", "A normal regular diet immediately", "High-protein solids only", "They must be fed through a stomach tube"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Because the jaw bones are cut and held only by plates, any heavy chewing forces will break the plates or displace the bone. A strict "no-chew" soft diet is mandatory during the bone healing phase.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with severe OSA has had a tracheostomy placed. How does the surgeon determine if they are ready to have the tracheostomy tube removed (decannulation) in the future?', '["If the patient stops snoring", "By performing a sleep study with the tracheostomy tube capped (plugged); if their AHI is normal and they have no respiratory distress, they can be decannulated", "If they lose 10 lbs", "If their voice returns", "Tracheostomy can never be removed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Capping the tracheostomy forces the patient to breathe normally through their upper airway. Performing a PSG while capped proves that the underlying airway has widened enough (due to weight loss or other interventions) to safely support normal breathing during sleep.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is "Base of Tongue Radiofrequency Ablation" (Somnoplasty)?', '["The use of a laser to burn the tongue", "A minimally invasive procedure where an electrode is inserted into the tongue base, delivering radiofrequency energy to heat and coagulate a small area of tissue. As it heals, it scars and shrinks, reducing tongue volume", "A surgical incision to remove the tongue", "The injection of alcohol", "A type of speech therapy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Somnoplasty is a low-morbidity, in-office or OR procedure. It requires multiple sessions to achieve significant tissue shrinkage, but is much better tolerated than open tongue resection.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which major arterial branch must be avoided during a midline glossectomy to prevent catastrophic hemorrhage?', '["The superior thyroid artery", "The Lingual Artery (which runs deep and lateral to the midline of the tongue base)", "The facial artery", "The internal carotid artery", "The ascending pharyngeal artery"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The paired lingual arteries run about 1.5 to 2 cm lateral to the midline of the tongue. Keeping the surgical cuts strictly in the midline (the fibrous septum of the tongue) is the primary safety rule to avoid massive arterial bleeding.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation surgery, where is the respiration sensing lead placed?', '["In the brainstem", "In the right second or third intercostal space, between the internal and external intercostal muscles", "Over the nose", "On the diaphragm", "In the stomach"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The sensing lead is placed between the rib muscles, where it detects the physical expansion and contraction of the chest wall during respiration, allowing the device to synchronize tongue stimulation with inspiration.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How does the Inspire device determine exactly when to deliver the electrical pulse to the hypoglossal nerve?', '["It fires continuously at 60 times a minute", "It uses the respiration sensing lead to detect the onset of inspiration, delivering a synchronized pulse to protrude the tongue only when the patient breathes in", "It is controlled by a remote control held by the bed partner", "It fires when the patient starts snoring", "It is on a simple timer"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Synchronized stimulation is highly efficient. It avoids fatiguing the tongue muscle and ensures the tongue is pushed forward exactly when air is being sucked into the lungs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with an Inspire implant presents for their 1-month activation. They report that the stimulation makes their tongue move, but it feels painful or uncomfortable. How is this managed?', '["The device must be surgically removed", "The voltage/current settings can be non-invasively adjusted using a programmer tablet to find the \"therapeutic window\" (the minimum voltage needed to open the airway without causing muscle pain)", "The patient must be sedated", "They are told to ignore the pain", "The wire is cut in the clinic"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Inspire is highly customizable. The clinician titrates the voltage (typically between 1.0 and 3.0 Volts) to find a setting that achieves good tongue protrusion without triggering painful muscle cramping.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the role of a "sleep remote" in Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation therapy?', '["To watch TV", "A handheld remote control given to the patient, allowing them to turn the device ON when going to bed, turn it OFF upon waking, or pause it if they need to get up in the night", "To adjust the CPAP pressure", "To call the ambulance", "To measure their weight"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The patient has full control. The remote has a "delay" button, so they can turn it on, and the device will wait 30 minutes before starting stimulation, allowing them to fall asleep naturally.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Why is a history of active psychiatric disease or severe cognitive impairment considered a relative contraindication for Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulation?', '["The device alters brain waves", "The therapy requires active patient participation, understanding, and compliance (e.g., using the remote, changing settings, managing the battery)", "Psychiatric medications block the device", "It causes depression", "It requires daily brain surgery"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Inspire is not passive. If a patient cannot safely operate the remote or understand when to turn it off, they are not a candidate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How long does the battery of an implanted Hypoglossal Nerve Stimulator typically last, and how is it replaced when it dies?', '["1 year; replaced in the clinic", "Approximately 8 to 11 years; replaced via a minor outpatient surgical procedure to exchange the Implantable Pulse Generator (IPG) under local anesthesia", "It lasts forever", "It is recharged through the skin every night", "It requires a major open chest surgery to replace"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The IPG battery life is excellent. When it runs low, a quick 30-minute incision over the chest pocket is made to swap out the generator, leaving the neck wires intact.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is "transpalatal advancement pharyngoplasty" (TPA)?', '["The removal of the hard palate", "A surgical procedure where a portion of the posterior hard palate is resected, allowing the soft palate to be advanced and anchored forward, widening the retropalatal space", "A skeletal surgery of the mandible", "A type of nasal surgery", "The placement of a metal stent in the throat"]'::jsonb, 1, 'TPA is a highly effective palatal surgery. By removing a sliver of the hard bone of the palate, the soft tissue can be slid forward and secured, significantly widening the upper airway without causing VPI.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which skeletal procedure involves cutting a circular piece of the anterior mandible and sliding it forward to advance the genial tubercle, without performing a full mandibular osteotomy?', '["Maxillomandibular advancement", "Genial Tubercle Advancement (GTA) or Genioglossus Advancement (GA)", "Bilateral sagittal split osteotomy", "Le Fort I osteotomy", "Condyle reconstruction"]'::jsonb, 1, 'GTA/GA is a targeted, less invasive skeletal procedure. It is often combined with soft tissue palatal surgery to address both the palate and tongue base levels in a single setting.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient undergoes MMA. On post-op day 1, they are noted to have severe, bilateral nasal airway obstruction. What is the most likely cause, and how is it managed?', '["The maxilla has collapsed", "Expected mucosal swelling and blood clots in the nasal cavity following the Le Fort I osteotomy; managed with head elevation, nasal saline sprays, gentle suctioning, and temporary nasal decongestants", "They have a new sinus infection", "The bones were placed incorrectly", "They need a tracheostomy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nasal congestion is universal after Le Fort I cuts. It is managed conservatively, and the patient must be strictly warned NOT to blow their nose to prevent orbital or subcutaneous emphysema.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Why is a patient who has undergone Maxillomandibular Advancement (MMA) strictly instructed NOT to blow their nose for the first 2-3 weeks post-operatively?', '["It will ruin their teeth", "Blowing the nose can force air and nasal bacteria through the Le Fort I osteotomy lines into the facial soft tissues or orbits, causing subcutaneous emphysema or severe facial infections", "It causes jaw fractures", "It causes vocal cord paralysis", "It will displace the CPAP mask"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is a critical post-op safety instruction. Blowing the nose creates high pressure that can push air through the unhealed bone cuts into the face or around the eyes (orbital emphysema).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is considered a primary, absolute indication for performing a Tracheostomy as the treatment for sleep apnea?', '["A patient who snore mildly", "Severe, life-threatening OSA with cor pulmonale or life-threatening arrhythmias, where all other medical and surgical options have failed or are contraindicated", "A patient who wants to stop wearing CPAP", "A patient with a normal sleep study", "A child with mild tonsil swelling"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Tracheostomy is the ultimate safety net. While rarely used today due to modern alternatives, it remains a vital, life-saving option for extreme, refractory cases.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the role of a "fenestrated" tracheostomy tube?', '["It is used to administer oxygen", "It has a small hole (fenestration) in the outer cannula that allows air to pass upward through the vocal cords when the inner cannula is removed and the tube is plugged, allowing the patient to speak normally", "It prevents all speaking", "It is only used in children", "It prevents mucus from escaping"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Fenestrated tubes are excellent for weaning and speech. By allowing air to flow up over the larynx, the patient can speak naturally while still having a secure airway.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with severe OSA has had a permanent tracheostomy placed. During the day, they wear a "speaking valve" on the tube. Can they wear this speaking valve when they go to sleep?', '["Yes, it is perfectly safe", "No, speaking valves should generally be removed at night, and the tracheostomy tube must be left fully open to ensure unimpeded breathing and airway protection during sleep", "Only if they sleep on their side", "Only if they use oxygen", "Yes, it prevents snoring"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Speaking valves are for awake, supervised use. At night, the valve is removed to ensure there is no risk of mechanical failure or mucus plugging that could cause suffocation during sleep.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is "tongue suspension" (e.g., the Repose system) in sleep surgery?', '["The surgical removal of the tongue", "A minimally invasive procedure where a permanent suture is threaded through the base of the tongue and anchored to a titanium screw in the chin, physically preventing the tongue from falling backward", "A type of neck brace", "A surgery to make the tongue longer", "A treatment for tongue cancer"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Tongue suspension is a quick, low-morbidity hypopharyngeal stabilizer. While less effective than GA or MMA over the long term, it can be a useful adjunct in multi-level surgery.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which major nerve provides the general sensory innervation to the posterior one-third of the tongue, and can be injured during aggressive tongue base reduction?', '["The Trigeminial nerve (CN V3)", "The Glossopharyngeal Nerve (CN IX)", "The Facial nerve (CN VII)", "The Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)", "The Vagus nerve"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) provides both taste and general sensation to the back third of the tongue. Injury during deep tongue base resection (like TORS) can cause permanent numbness and severe swallowing dysfunction.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with an Inspire device is scheduled for an MRI of their knee. What is the safety recommendation regarding MRIs for patients with this implant?', '["They can never have an MRI", "The modern Inspire system is MRI-conditional, meaning they can safely undergo MRIs of specific body parts under strict manufacturer guidelines (e.g., 1.5 Tesla scanners with the device turned off)", "They can have any MRI without precautions", "MRIs melt the device immediately", "Only open MRIs are allowed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Like modern pacemakers, Inspire is MRI-conditional. The radiologist and sleep surgeon must coordinate to ensure the scanner settings match the device safety profile, and the device must be placed in "MRI mode" before the scan.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the primary cause of death in patients with untreated, severe Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["Suffocation during a single apnea", "Cardiovascular events (myocardial infarction, sudden cardiac death, or ischemic stroke) driven by chronic vascular stress and hypoxia", "Pneumonia", "Lung cancer", "Accidental falls"]'::jsonb, 1, 'While patients rarely suffocate during a single apnea (because the brain always wakes up to breathe), the nightly, cumulative damage to the cardiovascular system eventually triggers a fatal heart attack or stroke.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How does the "Sher Staging System" categorize sleep surgery candidates?', '["Based on their age and weight", "Based on the anatomical site of obstruction: Phase I (soft tissue surgeries like UPPP/GA) and Phase II (skeletal surgeries like MMA for patients who failed Phase I)", "Based on their sleep study results only", "Based on their CPAP compliance", "Based on their nasal exam"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Sher paradigm was the original staging system for sleep surgery, establishing the concept of "staged" reconstruction (doing soft tissue first, and if that fails, moving to skeletal surgery).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the "apnea index" (AI) on a sleep study report?', '["The total number of central apneas only", "The average number of complete apneas (cessations of airflow) per hour of sleep", "The duration of the longest apnea", "The number of hypopneas", "The average oxygen drop"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The AI isolated the complete pauses in breathing. An AI > 5 is abnormal, and in severe patients, the AI alone can be over 30 or 40.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During an MMA surgery, what specialized dental splint is fabricated and placed between the teeth to hold the jaws in their pre-planned, advanced position while the titanium plates are screwed in?', '["A snore guard", "A Surgical Interocclusal Splint (wafer)", "A retainer", "A nightguard", "A crown"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The interocclusal splint (or wafer) is a custom plastic guide that locks the patient''s teeth into the perfect planned relationship during the bony cuts, ensuring the surgeon places the plates in the exact correct position to maintain their bite.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is "Distraction Osteogenesis" (MDO) of the mandible?', '["A surgery to remove the jaw", "A technique where the mandible is cut, and an adjustable metal device is attached across the cut. Over weeks, the device is turned to slowly separate the bone, forcing the body to grow new bone in the gap, advancing the jaw", "A type of dental implant", "A treatment for jaw cancer", "A way to stretch the tongue muscle only"]'::jsonb, 1, 'MDO is an elegant way to grow bone. It is widely used in pediatric craniofacial surgery to advance tiny jaws in infants, physically expanding the airway and avoiding the need for a tracheostomy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is considered a primary, long-term safety risk of a permanent, un-capped tracheostomy?', '["Vocal cord paralysis", "Chronic tracheal irritation, increased mucus production, tracheal stenosis, and a direct pathway for pathogens leading to recurrent bronchitis or pneumonia", "Loss of teeth", "Permanent taste loss", "Thyroid cancer"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A tracheostomy bypasses the nose and throat, which normally filter, warm, and humidify the air. Breathing dry, unfiltered air directly into the trachea leads to thick mucus plugging, tracheal scarring, and recurrent lung infections. Regular care and humidification are mandatory.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with severe OSA is undergoing multi-level surgery. The surgeon explains they will perform an ESP, a Genioglossus Advancement, and a Hyoid suspension. What is the primary benefit of combining these procedures?', '["It makes the surgery cheaper", "It addresses all potential sites of obstruction (palate, lateral walls, and tongue base) in a single operative setting, maximizing the chance of a surgical cure", "It reduces the recovery pain", "It avoids the need for general anesthesia", "It guarantees the patient will never need a sleep study again"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Multi-level surgery matches the complex, multi-site nature of adult sleep apnea. By clearing the entire airway column in one go, the success rate is significantly higher than doing UPPP alone.')
